Z||000000||AkursedX||07-17-2001||10:58 PM||||I'm debating running a heavier weight of oil for the summertime.  Especially since my break-down  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  I did a search on the topic, but I didn't find any sort of concrete info, so I decided to ask again.<P>Should I run 20w-50?  Or is that too heavy?  If so, what else should I run then?  <P>I drive my car fairly hard, but it's not like I drive like I'm at an autocross.  <P>And one other thing.  Does anyone know of a synthetic 20w-50 oil?  Does mobil make it?  Or anyone else?<P>Any input is greatly appreciated.<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||mindscape||07-17-2001||11:36 PM||||Up until my 1988 Fiero GT 2.8 died, 192K miles... since 1997 when I bought the car I have run Castrol FSX 5w50 year round; about 20K miles a year.  Though, I'm considering a switch after the rebuild.<P>Also consider that running a heavier weight oil reduces fuel economy a little since a thicker film of oil will result in slightly more drag.  Newer oils have better molecular cohesion and filming of metal surfaces.<P>Best advice is to run the same brand and change your oil religiously.  If you have a high mileage engine a heavier weight will cushion worn parts, thicker film.||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||07-18-2001||02:23 AM||||I run 10w30 Mobil 1 in my two 2.8 v6 cars, after blowing up a 2.5 duke *full* of Mobil 1 5w30 :)<P>One of the 2.8s is grounded with a SEVERE rod knock, and the other one might be developing one...    (both likely started before I bought the cars, I'm a pretty recent fiero-nut, ie last 2 years. and I don't commute.)<P><BR>In conclusion, rip out the engine the fiero came with and put something good in instead.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000003||V8Dreamer||07-18-2001||09:27 AM||||Go to <A HREF="http://www.howstuffworks.com" TARGET=_blank>www.howstuffworks.com</A>  and search for oil.  the article is entitled "More than you ever wanted to know about motor oil" never mind that, just use the link. <A HREF="http://www.howstuffworks.com/framed.htm?parent=question164.htm&url=http://www.vtr.org/maintain/oil-overview.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.howstuffworks.com/framed.htm?parent=question164.htm&url=http://www.vtr.org/maintain/oil-overview.html</A>   <BR>It explains what all the ratings mean, and also explains that the larger the gap between the numbers (ie 5w30 --&gt; 30-5=25) the more polymers added to the oil, which translates to more deposits yadda yadda yadda, basically, the less polymers, the better.<P>Ryan||||reg||1
